      "name": null,
      "problem": "Find the inverse of the following matrix: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the given matrix is diagonal.\n\nThe inverse of a diagonal matrix can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & {a}_{22} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & {a}_{nn}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{a}_{11}} & 0 & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{{a}_{22}} & ⋯ & 0 \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ⋯ & \\frac{1}{{a}_{nn}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIn our case, we obtain\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}={[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
